            type: "Sweet Cherry",
            name: "NY 38L",
            category: "Pearl cherry performance in Massachusetts",
            AKA: ["Burgundy Pearl", "BurgundyPearl (tm)"],
            harvest_time_unparsed: "Average pick June 30 in 2005-2011 observations, but often likely picked too early because of cracking and birds; best fruit quality harvest in Massachusetts surmised around July 7; target harvest week first week in July",
            description: "Fruit Notes identifies Burgundy Pearl as NY 38L from Bob Andersen's former Cornell stone fruit breeding program, evaluated on Gisela 6 rootstock at UMass Cold Spring Orchard. Source notes: early-season bloom in the May 6, 2011 bloom table, slightly ahead of Black Pearl and Ebony Pearl in bloom, heavy to very heavy annual yields, oblate-heart fruit shape, shorter stem than Black Pearl and Ebony Pearl, translucent attractive red skin, orange-red flesh, 9.1 g average fruit weight, 12.8 percent soluble solids, smaller than the 10 g target because of heavy crop loads and too-early harvest, and cracked in wet Massachusetts years despite outside literature describing excellent crack resistance"
